{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B4w11oHr3Q2odnhmmGMQ9F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:45:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Helm project (CNCF graduated)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D3 Application Delivery\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"helm\", \"chart\", \"values\", \"templates\", \"chart-repository\", \"release\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Helm — Charts (helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/)\n9\t\n10\tHelm uses a packaging format called charts. A chart is a collection of files that describe a related set of Kubernetes resources. A single chart might be used to deploy something simple, like a memcached pod, or something complex, like a full web app stack with HTTP servers, databases, caches, and so on. Charts are created as files laid out in a particular directory tree. They can be packaged into versioned archives to be deployed.\n11\t\n12\t## The chart file structure\n13\t- Chart.yaml — a YAML file containing information about the chart\n14\t- LICENSE — optional: a plain text file containing the license for the chart\n15\t- README.md — optional: a human-readable README file\n16\t- values.yaml — the default configuration values for this chart\n17\t- values.schema.json — optional: a JSON Schema for imposing a structure on the values.yaml file\n18\t- charts/ — a directory containing any charts upon which this chart depends\n19\t- crds/ — Custom Resource Definitions\n20\t- templates/ — a directory of templates that, when combined with values, will generate valid Kubernetes manifest files\n21\t- templates/NOTES.txt — optional: a plain text file containing short usage notes\n22\t\n23\tA chart repository is an HTTP server that houses one or more packaged charts, managed with the helm repo commands. An installed instance of a chart in a cluster is a release; the same chart can be installed many times, each creating a separately named release that can be upgraded and rolled back independently.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba092422-5310-41aa-b517-7e6c04f864c8","uuid":"cb24c5cd-b90d-43a7-a2e1-af2e895a0162","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:26:47.394Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/helm-charts-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:45:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Helm project (CNCF graduated)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D3 Application Delivery\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"helm\", \"chart\", \"values\", \"templates\", \"chart-repository\", \"release\"]\n---\n# Helm — Charts (helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/)\n\nHelm uses a packaging format called charts. A chart is a collection of files that describe a related set of Kubernetes resources. A single chart might be used to deploy something simple, like a memcached pod, or something complex, like a full web app stack with HTTP servers, databases, caches, and so on. Charts are created as files laid out in a particular directory tree. They can be packaged into versioned archives to be deployed.\n\n## The chart file structure\n- Chart.yaml — a YAML file containing information about the chart\n- LICENSE — optional: a plain text file containing the license for the chart\n- README.md — optional: a human-readable README file\n- values.yaml — the default configuration values for this chart\n- values.schema.json — optional: a JSON Schema for imposing a structure on the values.yaml file\n- charts/ — a directory containing any charts upon which this chart depends\n- crds/ — Custom Resource Definitions\n- templates/ — a directory of templates that, when combined with values, will generate valid Kubernetes manifest files\n- templates/NOTES.txt — optional: a plain text file containing short usage notes\n\nA chart repository is an HTTP server that houses one or more packaged charts, managed with the helm repo commands. An installed instance of a chart in a cluster is a release; the same chart can be installed many times, each creating a separately named release that can be upgraded and rolled back independently.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
