{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E4FDH53UmeAF2A3t4LS9Ka","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kustomize.io/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kustomize (Kubernetes SIG CLI)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D3 Application Delivery\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"kustomize\", \"template-free\", \"overlays\", \"kubectl-apply-k\", \"kustomization-yaml\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kustomize (kustomize.io)\n9\t\n10\tKubernetes native configuration management. Kustomize introduces a template-free way to customize application configuration that simplifies the use of off-the-shelf applications. Kustomize is built into kubectl as `apply -k`. It uses a purely declarative approach to configuration customization, encouraging a fork/modify/rebase workflow: a base directory holds the upstream manifests and a kustomization.yaml; overlays (for example dev, staging, prod) reference the base and layer patches, name prefixes/suffixes, labels, images, and generated ConfigMaps/Secrets on top — managing any number of distinctly customized Kubernetes configurations without forking the originals.\n11\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba092422-5310-41aa-b517-7e6c04f864c8","uuid":"7dd9b714-6628-4515-ade4-c26601e40a9f","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:26:52.824Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/kustomize-overview-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kustomize.io/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kustomize (Kubernetes SIG CLI)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D3 Application Delivery\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"kustomize\", \"template-free\", \"overlays\", \"kubectl-apply-k\", \"kustomization-yaml\"]\n---\n# Kustomize (kustomize.io)\n\nKubernetes native configuration management. Kustomize introduces a template-free way to customize application configuration that simplifies the use of off-the-shelf applications. Kustomize is built into kubectl as `apply -k`. It uses a purely declarative approach to configuration customization, encouraging a fork/modify/rebase workflow: a base directory holds the upstream manifests and a kustomization.yaml; overlays (for example dev, staging, prod) reference the base and layer patches, name prefixes/suffixes, labels, images, and generated ConfigMaps/Secrets on top — managing any number of distinctly customized Kubernetes configurations without forking the originals.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
