{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015SqmZVW8NTmLs565wqFdiC","type":"tool_result","content":"# Declarative Management of Kubernetes Objects Using Kustomize (Second Half)\n\n## Setting cross-cutting fields\n\nYou can set cross-cutting fields for all Kubernetes objects by using the `namespace`, `namePrefix`, `nameSuffix`, `labels`, and `annotations` fields in the kustomization file. Here's an example:\n\n```yaml\nnamespace: my-namespace\nnamePrefix: dev-\nnameSuffix: -v1\nlabels:\n  environment: dev\n  team: backend\nannotations:\n  note: \"This is a development environment\"\n```\n\nThese fields will be applied to all resources in the kustomization.\n\n## Composing and Customizing Resources\n\n### Composing\n\nKustomize supports composition of different resources. You can use the `resources` field in the kustomization file to include multiple resource files:\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n- deployment.yaml\n- service.yaml\n- configmap.yaml\n```\n\n### Customizing\n\nKustomize provides several ways to customize resources:\n\n#### Strategic Merge Patch\n\nUse strategic merge patch to add or override a few fields in a Kubernetes resource. A strategic merge patch is a partial specification that is merged into the existing resource. You can define strategic merge patches in a file and add it to the `patchesStrategicMerge` list. Here's an example:\n\n```yaml\n# deployment-patch.yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: my-app\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: app\n        image: my-app:v2\n```\n\nAdd it to the kustomization:\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n- deployment.yaml\npatchesStrategicMerge:\n- deployment-patch.yaml\n```\n\n#### JSON Patch\n\nA JSON patch is more powerful compared to a strategic merge patch. A JSON patch is a list of operations. Use a JSON patch when the target is not a native Kubernetes type or when you need list ordering. Here's an example:\n\n```yaml\n# kustomization.yaml\npatchesJson6902:\n- target:\n    group: apps\n    version: v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: my-app\n  patch: |-\n    - op: replace\n      path: /spec/replicas\n      value: 3\n    - op: add\n      path: /spec/template/spec/containers/0/env/-\n      value:\n        name: ENV_VAR\n        value: value\n```\n\n#### Images\n\nYou can change images in a kustomization without creating patches. Use the `images` field to list all images you want to replace:\n\n```yaml\nimages:\n- name: my-app\n  newName: my-app\n  newTag: v2\n```\n\n#### Replicas\n\nYou can override the number of replicas for Deployments and StatefulSets using the `replicas` field:\n\n```yaml\nreplicas:\n- name: my-app\n  count: 3\n```\n\n## Bases and Overlays\n\nKustomize supports a \"base and overlay\" concept. A **base** is a kustomization directory which contains a set of resources and a kustomization.yaml file. A base can be either a local directory or a directory from a remote repo. The **overlay** is a kustomization directory which references other kustomization directories and applies additional customization on top of them.\n\nHere's an example directory structure:\n\n```\nmy-app/\n├── base/\n│   ├── deployment.yaml\n│   ├── service.yaml\n│   └── kustomization.yaml\n├── overlays/\n│   ├── dev/\n│   │   ├── kustomization.yaml\n│   │   └── dev-patch.yaml\n│   └── prod/\n│       ├── kustomization.yaml\n│       └── prod-patch.yaml\n```\n\nThe `base/kustomization.yaml` contains:\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n- deployment.yaml\n- service.yaml\n```\n\nThe `overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml` contains:\n\n```yaml\nbases:\n- ../../base\npatchesStrategicMerge:\n- dev-patch.yaml\nnamePrefix: dev-\n```\n\nThe `overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml` contains:\n\n```yaml\nbases:\n- ../../base\npatchesStrategicMerge:\n- prod-patch.yaml\nnamePrefix: prod-\nreplicas:\n- name: my-app\n  count: 3\n```\n\n## How to apply/view/delete objects using Kustomize\n\nTo view resources found in a directory containing a kustomization file, run:\n\n```shell\nkubectl kustomize <kustomization_directory>\n```\n\nTo view the resources in a directory, run:\n\n```shell\nkubectl kustomize ./overlays/dev\n```\n\nTo apply those resources, run `kubectl apply` with `--kustomize` or `-k` flag:\n\n```shell\nkubectl apply -k <kustomization_directory>\n```\n\nTo check the status of the resources applied, run:\n\n```shell\nkubectl get -k <kustomization_directory>\n```\n\nTo describe resources, run:\n\n```shell\nkubectl describe -k <kustomization_directory>\n```\n\nTo delete resources applied via kustomization, run:\n\n```shell\nkubectl delete -k <kustomization_directory>\n```\n\n## Kustomize Feature List\n\nBelow is a non-exhaustive list of Kustomize features:\n\n| Feature | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `bases` | Specify a base kustomization to extend |\n| `resources` | Include Kubernetes resource files |\n| `configMapGenerator` | Generate ConfigMaps from files or literals |\n| `secretGenerator` | Generate Secrets from files or literals |\n| `generatorOptions` | Specify options for generators |\n| `namespace` | Set the namespace for all resources |\n| `namePrefix` | Add a prefix to resource names |\n| `nameSuffix` | Add a suffix to resource names |\n| `labels` | Add labels to all resources |\n| `annotations` | Add annotations to all resources |\n| `patchesStrategicMerge` | Apply strategic merge patches |\n| `patchesJson6902` | Apply JSON patches |\n| `images` | Customize image names and tags |\n| `replicas` | Override replica counts |\n| `commonLabels` | Add labels to all resources |\n| `commonAnnotations` | Add annotations to all resources |\n\n## What's next\n\n- Read the [Kustomize documentation](https://kustomize.io/)\n- See more [examples and use cases](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kustomize/tree/master/examples)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba092422-5310-41aa-b517-7e6c04f864c8","uuid":"8ccadddb-b6a9-433f-9871-e6233451bc36","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:28:47.883Z","tool_use_result":{"