{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0114Ko7vFwUxnbFdbHm1w8wz","type":"tool_result","content":"# Answers About Custom Resource Definitions Page\n\n1. **Two parts of a chart using custom resources:**\n\"There is a declaration of a CRD. This is the YAML file that has the kind `CustomResourceDefinition`. Then there are resources that _use_ the CRD.\"\n\n2. **Two methods for installing CRDs:**\n\"Method 1: Let `helm` Do It For You\" and \"Method 2: Separate Charts\"\n\n3. **Where CRD files go and templating:**\n\"There is now a special directory called `crds` that you can create in your chart to hold your CRDs. These CRDs are not templated, but will be installed by default when running a `helm install` for the chart.\"\n\n4. **Ordering of CRD installation:**\n\"For a CRD, the declaration must be registered before any resources of that CRDs kind(s) can be used.\"\n\n5. **Flag to skip CRD installation:**\n\"If you wish to skip the CRD installation step, you can pass the `--skip-crds` flag.\"\n\n6. **About upgrading or deleting CRDs:**\n\"There is no support at this time for upgrading or deleting CRDs using Helm.\"\n\n7. **About `--dry-run` and CRDs:**\n\"The `--dry-run` flag of `helm install` and `helm upgrade` is not currently supported for CRDs.\"\n\n8. **Method 2 recommendation and audience:**\n\"Put the CRD definition in one chart, and then put any resources that use that CRD in _another_ chart... this workflow may be more useful for cluster operators who have admin access to a cluster.\"\n\n9. **Code blocks:**\nNo directory-tree code blocks appear on this page."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba092422-5310-41aa-b517-7e6c04f864c8","uuid":"6596730c-3c96-4a6a-9402-f6160c1c0912","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:31:22.474Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":32934,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Answers About Custom Resource Definitions Page\n\n1. **Two parts of a chart using custom resources:**\n\"There is a declaration of a CRD. This is the YAML file that has the kind `CustomResourceDefinition`. Then there are resources that _use_ the CRD.\"\n\n2. **Two methods for installing CRDs:**\n\"Method 1: Let `helm` Do It For You\" and \"Method 2: Separate Charts\"\n\n3. **Where CRD files go and templating:**\n\"There is now a special directory called `crds` that you can create in your chart to hold your CRDs. These CRDs are not templated, but will be installed by default when running a `helm install` for the chart.\"\n\n4. **Ordering of CRD installation:**\n\"For a CRD, the declaration must be registered before any resources of that CRDs kind(s) can be used.\"\n\n5. **Flag to skip CRD installation:**\n\"If you wish to skip the CRD installation step, you can pass the `--skip-crds` flag.\"\n\n6. **About upgrading or deleting CRDs:**\n\"There is no support at this time for upgrading or deleting CRDs using Helm.\"\n\n7. **About `--dry-run` and CRDs:**\n\"The `--dry-run` flag of `helm install` and `helm upgrade` is not currently supported for CRDs.\"\n\n8. **Method 2 recommendation and audience:**\n\"Put the CRD definition in one chart, and then put any resources that use that CRD in _another_ chart... this workflow may be more useful for cluster operators who have admin access to a cluster.\"\n\n9. **Code blocks:**\nNo directory-tree code blocks appear on this page.","durationMs":5039,"url":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_best_practices/custom_resource_definitions/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ShdzsKU9pRMVqJB7tfgL7K","type":"tool_result","content":"# Answers from Helm Chart Repository Guide\n\n**1. Chart Repository Definition:**\n\"A chart repository is an HTTP server that houses an `index.yaml` file and optionally some packaged charts.\"\n\n**2. HTTP Server Capabilities:**\n\"Because a chart repository can be any HTTP server that can serve YAML and tar files and can answer GET requests, you have a plethora of options when it comes down to hosting your own chart repository.\"\n\n**3. Upload Limitations:**\nThe page does not contain any sentence stating that charts cannot be uploaded to a repository server or that there are no server-side APIs for this purpose. It discusses uploading charts but doesn't explicitly state limitations on server-side upload functionality.\n\n**4. Index.yaml Description and Example:**\n\"The index file is a yaml file called `index.yaml`. It contains some metadata about the package, including the contents of a chart's `Chart.yaml` file.\"\n\nExample (reproduced exactly as shown):\n```\napiVersion: v1\nentries:\n  alpine:\n    - created: 2016-10-06T16:23:20.499814565-06:00\n      description: Deploy a basic Alpine Linux pod\n      digest: 99c76e403d752c84ead610644d4b1c2f2b453a74b921f422b9dcb8a7c8b559cd\n      home: https://helm.sh/helm\n      name: alpine\n      sources:\n      - https://github.com/helm/helm\n      urls:\n      - https://technosophos.github.io/tscharts/alpine-0.2.0.tgz\n      version: 0.2.0\n```\n[continued with remaining entries...]\n\n**5. Repository Directory Layout:**\n```\ncharts/\n  |\n  |- index.yaml\n  |\n  |- alpine-0.1.2.tgz\n  |\n  |- alpine-0.1.2.tgz.prov\n```\n\n**6. Command Descriptions and Examples:**\n- \"Charts in a chart repository must be packaged (`helm package chart-name/`)\"\n- Example: `$ helm package docs/examples/alpine/`\n- Example: `$ helm repo index fantastic-charts --url https://fantastic-charts.storage.googleapis.com`\n- Example: `$ helm repo add fantastic-charts https://fantastic-charts.storage.googleapis.com`\n\n**7. Static Site Hosting:**\n\"You have options including Google Cloud Storage (GCS) bucket, Amazon S3 bucket, GitHub Pages, or even create your own web server.\" The page extensively covers GitHub Pages and GCS as hosting options."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba092422-5310-41aa-b517-7e6c04f864c8","uuid":"e959f9ea-63ae-44c4-a131-8c32d61a9f1a","timestamp":"2026-08-31T08:31:30.313Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":62459,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Answers from Helm Chart Repository Guide\n\n**1. Chart Repository Definition:**\n\"A chart repository is an HTTP server that houses an `index.yaml` file and optionally some packaged charts.\"\n\n**2. HTTP Server Capabilities:**\n\"Because a chart repository can be any HTTP server that can serve YAML and tar files and can answer GET requests, you have a plethora of options when it comes down to hosting your own chart repository.\"\n\n**3. Upload Limitations:**\nThe page does not contain any sentence stating that charts cannot be uploaded to a repository server or that there are no server-side APIs for this purpose. It discusses uploading charts but doesn't explicitly state limitations on server-side upload functionality.\n\n**4. Index.yaml Description and Example:**\n\"The index file is a yaml file called `index.yaml`. It contains some metadata about the package, including the contents of a chart's `Chart.yaml` file.\"\n\nExample (reproduced exactly as shown):\n```\napiVersion: v1\nentries:\n  alpine:\n    - created: 2016-10-06T16:23:20.499814565-06:00\n      description: Deploy a basic Alpine Linux pod\n      digest: 99c76e403d752c84ead610644d4b1c2f2b453a74b921f422b9dcb8a7c8b559cd\n      home: https://helm.sh/helm\n      name: alpine\n      sources:\n      - https://github.com/helm/helm\n      urls:\n      - https://technosophos.github.io/tscharts/alpine-0.2.0.tgz\n      version: 0.2.0\n```\n[continued with remaining entries...]\n\n**5. Repository Directory Layout:**\n```\ncharts/\n  |\n  |- index.yaml\n  |\n  |- alpine-0.1.2.tgz\n  |\n  |- alpine-0.1.2.tgz.prov\n```\n\n**6. Command Descriptions and Examples:**\n- \"Charts in a chart repository must be packaged (`helm package chart-name/`)\"\n- Example: `$ helm package docs/examples/alpine/`\n- Example: `$ helm repo index fantastic-charts --url https://fantastic-charts.storage.googleapis.com`\n- Example: `$ helm repo add fantastic-charts https://fantastic-charts.storage.googleapis.com`\n\n**7. Static Site Hosting:**\n\"You have options including Google Cloud Storage (GCS) bucket, Amazon S3 bucket, GitHub Pages, or even create your own web server.\" The page extensively covers GitHub Pages and GCS as hosting options.","durationMs":8216,"url":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/chart_repository/"}}
